operly is often the fix. We should guide them to install the Guest Additions from the VirtualBox menu and not from the Ubuntu repository.\n\nBest steps to fix:\n\n1. **Upgrade Virtual Box**: Ensure the user is using the latest VirtualBox version that still supports Ubuntu 10. However, note that Ubuntu 10.04 is EOL and might not be fully supported by new VirtualBox versions. But if possible, updating might fix known bugs.\n\n2. **Reinstall Guest Additions properly**:\n   - Boot the Ubuntu guest.\n   - In VirtualBox menu, select \"Devices\" -> \"Insert Guest Additions CD image\".\n   - Mount the CD in Ubuntu (if not auto-mounted).\n   - Run: `sudo sh /media/VBOXADDITIONS_*/VBoxLinuxAdditions.run`\n   - After installing, reboot.\n\n3. **Check kernel headers**: For the Guest Additions to build the kernel modules, the kernel headers must be installed. On Ubuntu 10.04, that would be:\n   ```bash\n   sudo apt-get install build-essential linux-headers-$(uname -r)\n   ```\n   Then reinstall Guest Additions.\n\n4. **Check log for errors**: The user can run `dmesg | grep vbox` after a failed mount to see kernel messages about the issue.\n\n5. **Alternative Mount Command**: Use `mount -t vboxsf -o rw,uid=1000,gid=1000 Test /mnt/test2` to mount with specific user permissions.\n\n6. **Use a different shared folder name**: Avoid spaces and special characters. In this case, \"Test\" is fine, but try a simpler one.\n\nSince the user has tried several steps, I think the best next option is to ensure Guest Additions are properly compiled for the current kernel. This involves installing kernel headers and reinstalling Guest Additions.\n\nFinal Answer: Guide the user to install the necessary dependencies and reinstall Guest Additions.\n\nBut wait: Ubuntu 10.04 is very old. The Guest Additions might not properly install due to missing dependencies or outdated components. Therefore, we need to be cautious and provide steps for an old system.\n\nAnother idea: The virtual machine might have multiple network adapters? Disabling unnecessary ones might help.\n\nBut the most common solution is Guest Additions.\n\nI think we should advise:\n\n- Install the required packages: `build-essential` and the current kernel headers.\n- Reinstall Guest Additions.\n\nSo, the step-by-step guide is:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt-get update\nsudo apt-get install build-essential linux-headers-$(uname -r)\nsudo /etc/init.d/vboxadd setup   # This rebuilds the modules\n```\n\nIf that fails, then try a reboot.\n\nIf still failing, try adding the user to the vboxsf group to avoid using sudo:\n  sudo usermod -a -G vboxsf $USER\n\nBut the user has tried root so permission might not be the issue.\n\nAlternatively, try mounting at boot via /etc/fstab? But let's not complicate now.\n\nThus, the primary solution is rebuilding Guest Additions.\n</think>\nBased on the error and your troubleshooting steps, the \"Protocol error\" typically stems from mismatched or improperly configured VirtualBox Guest Additions, especially with older Linux systems. Here's a focused solution:\n\n### Key Issue:\nUbuntu 10 uses Linux kernel 2.6.32, which has compatibility issues with newer VirtualBox versions. The Guest Additions kernels module (`vboxsf`) fails to load correctly.\n\n### Recommended Solution:\n1. **Reinstall Guest Additions Correctly**:\n   - Uninstall existing additions:\n     ```bash\n     sudo /opt/VBoxGuestAdditions-*/uninstall.sh  # If exists\n     sudo apt-get remove virtualbox-qq-guest-additions  # Legacy package\n     ```\n   - Install **dependencies**:\n     ```bash\n     sudo apt-get update\n     sudo apt-get install build-essential module-assistant linux-headers-$(uname -r)\n     sudo m-a prepare\n     ```\n   - Insert Guest Additions CD image via VirtualBox menu:  \n     *Devices \u2192 Insert Guest Additions CD Image*\n   - Mount and install manually:\n     ```bash\n     sudo mount /dev/cdrom /media/cdrom\n     cd /media/cdrom\n     sudo sh VBoxLinuxAdditions.run --nox11  # No X11 mode\n     ```\n\n2. **Force Reload Kernel Module**:\n   ```bash\n   sudo modprobe -a vboxsf  # Check if loaded\n   sudo /etc/init.d/vboxadd setup  # Rebuild module\n   ```\n\n3. **Verify Share Configuration**:\n   - Ensure the VirtualBox share is named exactly **`Test`** (case-sensitive).\n   - Confirm `/mnt/test2` exists: `sudo mkdir -p /mnt/test2`\n   - Mount with explicit options to bypass permission issues:\n     ```bash\n     sudo mount -t vboxsf -o uid=1000,gid=1000 Test /mnt/test2\n     ```\n     *(Replace `1000` with your user's UID/GID)*\n\n4. **Workaround if Persistent**:  \n   If still failing, downgrade VirtualBox to **version 5.2** (last with confirmed Ubuntu 10.04 support):\n   - Uninstall current VirtualBox on Windows\n   - Install [VirtualBox 5.2](https://download.virtualbox.org/virtualbox/5.2.44/)  \n   - Repeat Guest Additions installation inside VM\n\n### Post-Installation:\n- **Reboot**: `sudo reboot`\n- **Verify Mount**: `df -h` should show `/mnt/test2`\n\n<answer>\nReinstall Guest Additions with dependencies, rebuild kernel modules, and ensure UID/GID are explicitly specified during mount. If unresolved, downgrade VirtualBox to version 5.2.\n</answer>"}], "q_num": 87, "originalconversations": ["I have Ubuntu 10 as the guest OS on a Windows 7 machine.  I have been trying to setup shares through VirtualBox, but nothing is working.  First, I create the share in VirtualBox and point it to a Windows folder.  Then I try to mount the drive in Linux, but I keep getting\n```/sbin/mount.vboxsf: mounting failed with the error: Protocol error\n```\nI have read so many solutions to this, but none seem to work.  I have tried:\n\n* Using the mount.vboxsf syntax\n* Reinstalling VBox additions\n* Rebooting\n* Enabling and trying as root account\n\nI made a share called &quot;Test&quot; in VBox Shared folders.  Then I made a directory in ubuntu named &quot;test2&quot;.  Then I tried to execute this command:\n```sudo mount -t vboxsf Test /mnt/test2\n```\nAny other ideas?", "In order to use shared folder functionality few prerequisites need to be met:\n\n* Make sure that Guest Additions are properly installed on the guest OS.\n* Users in a guest Ubuntu must be in the group ```vboxsf``` to be able to access shares.\n* Define a directory on the host that will be used in the virtual machine using the settings dialogue of Virtual Box.\n\nDepending on host or guest OS the following may also be needed:\n\n* Do not share personal folders like ```/home/username``` or ```My Documents```\n* Avoid special characters or empty spaces in the path to the shared folder, especially if the host or VM is a Windows machine\n* Use different names for share and mountpoint\n* Create a mountpoint on the guest OS (best in your HOME directory).\n\nTesting shared folders functionality can be done by creating a shared directory on the host (e.g. ```C:\\myshare```), define this as a shared folder for your guest system in Virtual Box settings (e.g. ```share```), create a mount point in your guest os (e.g.  ```mkdir /home/username/host```) and mount this in the guest OS with the command:\n```sudo mount -t vboxsf -o uid=1000,gid=1000 share /home/username/host\n```\nwhere the option -o makes sure that you will have access to the mount (that will otherwise be owned by root).\nFor further information on shared folders see also the Virtual Box User Manual.\nFiles or directories can also be shared over the network by using Samba on the host and the guest."], "originalsource": "stackexchange", "originalprompt": "I have Ubuntu 10 as the guest OS on a Windows 7 machine.  I have been trying to setup shares through VirtualBox, but nothing is working.  First, I create the share in VirtualBox and point it to a Windows folder.  Then I try to mount the drive in Linux, but I keep getting\n```/sbin/mount.vboxsf: mounting failed with the error: Protocol error\n```\nI have read so many solutions to this, but none seem to work.  I have tried:\n\n* Using the mount.vboxsf syntax\n* Reinstalling VBox additions\n* Rebooting\n* Enabling and trying as root account\n\nI made a share called &quot;Test&quot; in VBox Shared folders.  Then I made a directory in ubuntu named &quot;test2&quot;.  Then I tried to execute this command:\n```sudo mount -t vboxsf Test /mnt/test2\n```\nAny other ideas?"}
