urs given -> This does not necessarily mean [1..1] by itself. We have to consider minOccurs. If no occurrence attributes are given at all, then minOccurs defaults to 1 and maxOccurs defaults to 1, so it's [1..1]. But the question only says \"maxOccurs\", but note: the user wrote \"No maxOccurs given\" without mentioning minOccurs. So we assume no occurrence indicators at all? Actually, the user says \"No maxOccurs given\" but might mean that minOccurs is present? Let me read the question again.\n\nThe user states:\n\n```\nNo maxOccurs given -> Is this the cardinality [1..1]?\n```\n\nIn XML Schema, if maxOccurs is not specified, it defaults to 1. However, minOccurs also has a default of 1. Therefore, if no occurrence attributes are given, it's [1..1]. But if only minOccurs is given and no maxOccurs, then maxOccurs defaults to 1. So regardless, unless minOccurs is set to 0, the cardinality would be [minOccurs..1] and if minOccurs is not given, it's 1.\n\nBut note: the user only mentioned maxOccurs. So I think they are referring to the situation where maxOccurs is not present. In any case, the specification says that maxOccurs defaults to 1.\n\nTherefore, the answer for Case 1: Yes, if no occurrence attributes are given or if maxOccurs is omitted and minOccurs is given (and then maxOccurs defaults to 1), then the cardinality has max=1. But note: minOccurs default is 1, so unless changed, it's [1..1].\n\nCase 2: They show an empty block and say \"I suppose this is simply invalid\". What does an empty block mean? They wrote:\n\n```\n```\n\nIt seems they might be referring to an element declaration without any occurrence indicators? Or perhaps without any content? Actually, in the context, they have three separate cases. The second case is represented as an empty code block? But note that in the message it's:\n\n```\n```\n\nSo that might be a typo or misinterpretation. Alternatively, they might be referring to:\n\nCase 2: What if there is neither minOccurs nor maxOccurs? 