bj = new object();\n\nvoid CriticalSectionMethod()\n{\n    lock (_lockObj)  // Enters critical section\n    {\n        // Shared resource access\n    }  // Automatically exits\n}\n```\n**Characteristics**:\n- Process-confined\n- Microsecond-level latency\n- No cross-process capability\n- Automatic release via scope exit\n\n#### Mutexes in C#:\n- Implemented via `Mutex` class  \n  (designed for *inter-process* sync)\n```csharp\nusing var mutex = new Mutex(false, \"Global\\\\MyAppMutex\");\n\nvoid MutexProtectedMethod()\n{\n    if (mutex.WaitOne(5000))  // Wait 5 sec max\n    {\n        try\n        {\n            // Access shared resource\n        }\n        finally\n        {\n            mutex.ReleaseMutex();  // Explicit release\n        }\n    }\n    else throw new TimeoutException();\n}\n```\n**Characteristics**:\n- Global scope with `\"Global\\\\\"` prefix (Windows) or `/` prefix (Linux)\n- Supports mutex abandon detection\n- Requires \u22481,000 CPU cycles per wait/release\n- Can coordinate Docker containers/machines via names\n\n---\n\n### When Do These Differences Matter in C#?\n1. **Performance Critical Sections**:  \n   Use `lock`/`Monitor` (critical section equivalent) for:\n   - High-throughput financial apps\n   - Game loop synchronization\n   - Memory cache access\n\n2. **Cross-Boundary Synchronization**:  \n   Use `Mutex` when:\n   - Coordinating between multiple processes (e.g., installer conflicts)\n   - Shared file access across unrelated apps\n   - Cloud-hosted apps needing machine-level locking\n\n---\n\n### Key Decision Factors:\n| Purpose               | Critical Section (`lock`) | Mutex          |\n|-----------------------|---------------------------|----------------|\n| **Speed Critical**    | \u2705 Optimal               | \u274c Avoid       |\n| **Single-Process**    | \u2705 Yes                   | Possible but slow |\n| **Multi-Process**     | \u274c Impossible            | \u2705 Required    |\n| **Named Resource Sync**| \u274c No       